{"id":"loan-borrowers","title":"Who the City lends housing money to","question":"Which borrowers received the City’s housing loans since fiscal 2019, and how concentrated is the lending once sister partnerships are counted as one owner?","finding":"The payment file shows $2.62 billion of housing loans paid out from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date. Counting partnerships the files tie to one owner as a single borrower, the five largest received 36%: Mercy Housing California ($351.5 million, 21 partnerships); Bridge Housing Corp ($250.8 million, 8 partnerships); Mission Economic Development Agency ($170.4 million, 4 partnerships); Chinatown Community Development Center ($99.5 million, 10 partnerships); 1064 Mission LP ($75.1 million). $134.0 million more was paid to title and escrow companies at closings, where the file does not show the borrower. Each development borrows through its own partnership, so the list of payees is long (154 names) while the list of owners is short.","how":"Payments by the Mayor’s office accounts (which hold the housing office) and the Office of Community Investment and Infrastructure on the ledger lines “Loans Issued By City” and “Grants & Forgivable Loans”, added up by payee. Payees that this project’s owner-groups tie to one owner on record evidence (the same business account, or a related-organization schedule on a tax return) are added together and shown under the name that the filings give most often as the parent; a partnership whose name is that parent’s full name followed by a number is added to it as well. Board files are loan resolutions since 2015 whose short title names one of the group’s partnerships. A payment is dated by its voucher number; one that belongs to a year before fiscal 2019 is shown as 2018.","limits":"A separate partnership for each building is how every loan in the Board’s files is set up. The owner-groups come from registrations and tax schedules; a partnership with no such record is shown alone and the true concentration is higher than shown. The file shows money leaving the City, not the balance now owed or anything repaid. What would settle it: the housing office’s portfolio list by sponsor (loans, balances and repayments per development). The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"borrower","t":"Borrower, or the owner its partnerships are tied to","type":"org"},{"k":"kind","t":"Kind of payee","type":"text"},{"k":"names","t":"Partnerships paid","type":"int"},{"k":"lent","t":"Paid out, fiscal 2019–2027","type":"usd"},{"k":"years","t":"Years with payments","type":"int"},{"k":"first","t":"First year","type":"year"},{"k":"last","t":"Latest year","type":"year"},{"k":"largest","t":"Largest partnership","type":"text"},{"k":"largestLent","t":"Paid to it","type":"usd"},{"k":"boardFiles","t":"Board loan files naming them","type":"int"},{"k":"filesAmending","t":"Of which amend an earlier loan","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"borrower":"Mercy Housing California","borrowerKey":"MERCY HOUSING CALIFORNIA","kind":"borrowers tied to one owner in the files","names":21,"lent":351515671,"years":10,"first":"2018","last":"2027","largest":"Mercy Housing California 97 L.P.","largestLent":82074414,"boardFiles":1,"filesAmending":1},{"borrower":"Bridge Housing Corp","b